      "text": "For $\\triangle QSP$, we have $\\angle QSP + \\angle SQR + \\angle PQS = 180^\\circ$.  Substituting  $80^\\circ$ for $\\angle QSP$, $\\angle SQR + \\angle PQS = 100^\\circ$.  Thus $\\angle PQS = 20^\\circ$.Ray has difficulty reading this transcribed question: Create a question with image and text.\n\nIn the diagram, $\\angle RQO$ and $\\angle RPO$ are vertical angles.  Find the value of $x$.\n\n\n(A)  $8^\\circ$  \n(B)  $52^\\circ$  \n(C)  $55^\\circ$  \n(D)  $84^\\circ$  \n(E)  $100^\\circ$\n\nSolution:  \nVertical angles are the angles opposite each other when two lines cross.  They are of equal measure.  Since $\\angle RQO$,$\\angle RPO$, and $\\angle QPO$ are vertical angles, $\\angle RQO = \\angle RPO = x^\\circ$.Because $QPO$ and $RPO$ are adjacent angles that form a straight line, sum of their measures must be $180^\\circ$, or $2x = 180^\\circ$, so $x = 90^\\circ$.Since $PQO$ and $RPO$ are vertical angles, $\\angle RPO = 90^\\circ$. Because $PQO$ is a right angle and $QPO$ is an isolate angle, $x = 84^\\circ.$Ray has difficulty reading this transcribed question: Create a question with image and text.\n\nThe diagram shows a sector of a circle with a radius of 10 units.  Trisection lines are drawn from D to F and from E to F.  What is x?\n[asy]\nimport graph; size(8.36cm); \nreal labelscalefactor = 0.5; /* changes label-to-point distance */\npen dps = linewidth(0.7) + fontsize(10); defaultpen(dps); /* default pen style */ \npen dotstyle = black; /* point style */ \nreal xmin = -3.89, xmax = 9.53, ymin = -2.3, ymax = 7.07;\n\ndraw((1.92,1.76)--(3.92,2.26)--(9.53,2.26)--(6.59,5.71)--(3.59,5.71)--(2.92,4.2)--(3.59,3.59)--(4.68,4.68)--(7.02,6.03)--(6.59,7.03)--(4.68,6.03)--(3.92,7.25)--(2.92,6.68)--(3.59,5.71)--(11.18,5.71)--cycle, linewidth(2.21) + red);\n\ndraw((0.65,4.89)--(2.11,4.64)--(8.97,4.64)--(3.78,0.55) --(4.96,1.22) --(2.98,1.83)--cycle, linewidth(2.21) + red);\n\ndraw((1.66,4.58)--(3.78,4.64)--(8.02,2.93)--(9.2,0.55)--(10.35,1.22)--(8.02,